{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2016",
    "module": "commonjs",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "outDir": "./dist",
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitAny": true,
    "strictNullChecks": true,
    "strictFunctionTypes": true,
    "strictPropertyInitialization": true,
    "noImplicitThis": true,
    "alwaysStrict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": false,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "downlevelIteration": true
  },
  // ts-node (knex loads migrations and seeds through it) does not honour
  // "include", so src/types.d.ts — which augments Express.Request with `user` —
  // never entered its program. Any migration importing application code that
  // reaches src/database/audit-context.ts then failed to compile with
  // TS2339 "Property 'user' does not exist on type Request". That made
  // `migrate:latest` from a fresh database impossible, and it was invisible
  // only because the one migration that imports that chain was already
  // applied everywhere, so knex never re-required it.
  //
  // "files": true loads the tsconfig's own files/include into ts-node's
  // program. It lives here rather than in package.json because production runs
  // migrations as `docker exec ... npx knex migrate:latest`, which never sees
  // an npm script.
  // knex loads migrations and seeds through ts-node. Two problems, one fix:
  //
  // 1. tsconfig "exclude"s migrations/ and ts-node ignores "include", so
  //    src/types.d.ts's Express.Request augmentation never entered its program
  //    and any migration importing src/database/audit-context.ts failed with
  //    TS2339 "Property 'user' does not exist on type Request".
  // 2. "files": true fixed that by loading all of src/**/* into the checker —
  //    and OOM'd the 1GB production box ("FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit").
  //
  // transpileOnly skips type checking altogether, which is the right trade for
  // migrations: correctness is already enforced by `npm run build` /
  // `tsc --noEmit` before anything ships, and a migration process has no
  // business type-checking the whole application at runtime.
  //
  // It lives here rather than in package.json because production runs
  // migrations as `docker exec ... npx knex migrate:latest`, which never sees
  // an npm script.
  "ts-node": { "transpileOnly": true },
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "knexfile.ts",
    "knexfile.js",
    "migrations",
    "seeds"
  ]
}
